  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 AM MST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nThe weather pattern across eastern Idaho will remain generally \nbenign through the workweek, characterized by an absence of \nhigh-impact systems and continued above-normal temperatures. \nWeak upper-level ridging over the area will slowly recede today \nas an upper-level shortwave digs into the Pacific Northwest and \ntracks eastward along the US/Canada border. Moisture with this \nsystem is very limited, though high-resolution convective- \nallowing models (CAMs) indicate a low-to-moderate potential to \nsqueeze out an isolated light shower or mountain flurry across \nthe Central Mountains and near Island Park late this evening \nthrough early Tuesday morning. The primary impact will be an \nincrease in mid-to-high level cloud cover regionwide. As the \nshortwave advances into the Northern Plains on Tuesday, skies \nwill rapidly clear from west to east.\n\nSkies will remain mostly clear as high pressure rebuilds and \ncenters over the Northern Great Basin from Wednesday through \nFriday. Strong subsidence and rising heights will promote \nunseasonably warm afternoons, with daytime highs running 7 to 12\ndegrees above seasonal averages. Valley locations across the \nSnake River Plain and Magic Valley will reach the upper 50s to \nlower 60s under light winds, making for very pleasant late- \nautumn conditions.\n\nThe synoptic pattern begins to break down over the weekend as \nan upper-level trough over the Gulf of Alaska taps into Pacific \nmoisture and ejects energy into the Pacific Northwest. While \ndeep moisture will take time to reach eastern Idaho, the \nregional pressure gradient will tighten over the weekend, \nresulting in increasingly breezy surface winds alongside \nthickening cloud cover. Unseasonably mild temperatures will hold\nthrough Sunday afternoon ahead of the front. The primary \nmoisture plume and associated cold front are progged to arrive \nlate Sunday night into Monday. A sharp 500 mb height drop will \ndrive a 10 to 15-degree temperature plunge for early next week, \naccompanied by scattered to widespread rain and snow showers. \nWhile timing details will be refined as the system approaches, \nquiet conditions will prevail across the area until then.",
